from pathlib import Path
import re, shutil, hashlib, json

ROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parent
SRC=ROOT/'si_narrative_revision'
OUT=ROOT/'narrative_literature_revision'
OUT.mkdir(exist_ok=True)
(OUT/'evidence').mkdir(exist_ok=True)
shutil.copytree(SRC/'figures',OUT/'figures',dirs_exist_ok=True)
s=(SRC/'AbDevX_SI_discussion_main_SI.tex').read_text(encoding='utf-8')

abstract=r'''\begin{abstract}
\noindent\textbf{Background.} Early antibody selection requires balancing molecular interaction behavior, stability, and expression. Protein language models and physicochemical descriptors offer complementary information, but combining them does not consistently improve predictions when assay labels are scarce and candidate populations change. We asked whether assay-specific construction of complementary experts could improve external antibody ranking without training a complex final gate.

\noindent\textbf{Results.} AbDev-X combines source selection, assay-related descriptor masking, two-stream stacking, and mechanistic feature-group prediction through an equal-weight average. Models were developed on GDPa1 (246 antibodies) using cluster--isotype cross-validation and evaluated on the external GDPa3 panel (80 antibodies; assay-dependent label availability). Across HIC, AC-SINS, PR-CHO, SMAC, and HAC, AbDev-X achieved the highest mean Spearman correlation among nine evaluated strategies: $.472$, compared with $.452$ for the strongest individual expert, $.307$ for ESM-2 Ridge, and $.273$ for AbLang2 Ridge. This five-assay grouping is descriptive of the observed results. Paired exploratory intervals supported gains over both PLM baselines on HIC, SMAC, and HAC. Source removal connected the gains to assay-dependent contributions, including structural bio features for HIC and SMAC and antibody embeddings for AC-SINS. Across all ten endpoints, mean correlation was $.249$; Tm2 and SEC remained weaknesses, including against published reference points.

\noindent\textbf{Conclusions.} Complementary assay-aware predictors improve aggregate ranking in the evaluated five-assay setting, with the clearest matched-baseline gains on hydrophobic and surface-interaction endpoints. The broader evaluation identifies where additional assay-specific learning is required. AbDev-X provides a framework for candidate prioritization that retains assay-level evidence and uncertainty rather than collapsing developability into one universal score.
\end{abstract}'''
s=re.sub(r'\\begin\{abstract\}.*?\\end\{abstract\}',lambda _:abstract,s,count=1,flags=re.S)

background=r'''\section{Background}
Selecting a therapeutic antibody requires more than identifying a strong binder. A sequence with desirable target recognition can still be difficult to express, purify, concentrate, or formulate. Early discovery must therefore compare candidates across several developability properties while experimental material and assay capacity are limited. Clinical-antibody profiling and the Therapeutic Antibody Profiler establish the importance of evaluating multiple physicochemical attributes rather than relying on a single warning threshold~\citep{jain2017,raybould2019tap}. A useful predictor would help decide which candidates merit further measurement and which liabilities require closer inspection, without allowing one favorable endpoint to conceal another unfavorable one.

Sequence modeling has changed what can be learned before an antibody is produced. General protein language models have guided efficient experimental antibody affinity maturation~\citep{hie2024}; frozen ESM-2 and antibody-specific AbLang2 representations also make pretrained sequence information available to small supervised datasets~\citep{lin2023esm2,ablang2}. However, evolutionary sequence regularity is not itself a measurement of hydrophobic retention, self-association, or expression. Named descriptors and predicted structures offer a complementary route by exposing charge, composition, solvent accessibility, and surface-patch properties. PROPERMAB already demonstrates the value of integrating sequence and structure-derived molecular features~\citep{propermab2025}. The remaining question is how to use those sources selectively when their relevance changes with the assay.

Recent evidence identifies two obstacles. First, a richer representation need not provide a better assay predictor. Jiang\,et\,al. found that hybrid descriptor--embedding models did not consistently outperform descriptor-only baselines on GDPa external evaluation~\citep{jiang2026}. Amini\,et\,al. studied PLM adaptation and generalization on industrial antibody panels, emphasizing the importance of sequence-family separation and training composition~\citep{amini2026}. The FLAb2 preprint likewise reports inconsistent developability prediction across heterogeneous datasets~\citep{flab2}. These findings leave open whether selective, complementary use of sources can be more useful than committing every assay to the same representation or integration rule.

Second, internal performance can overstate the ranking that transfers to new antibodies. The PROPHET-Ab platform provides GDPa1 measurements on 246 antibodies~\citep{prophet2025}, and the Ginkgo competition adds a subsequent 80-antibody GDPa3 panel characterized on the same platform~\citep{ginkgo2026}. Substantial CV-to-held-out discrepancies in the competition demonstrate that standardized measurement alone does not remove the generalization problem. Context-aware approaches such as CA-MAP address a related challenge through example-conditioned property prediction and simulated batch shifts, but its computational-property evaluation differs from external prediction of these experimental assays~\citep{camap2026}. Progress therefore requires explicit evaluation on the intended assay and candidate population, as well as an explanation of which information survives the population change.

We designed AbDev-X around the hypothesis that assay specificity should enter before the final ensemble average. Four experts make different use of the same frozen representations: selecting one source, restricting descriptor families, stacking two predictive streams, or combining eligible mechanistic groups. This construction addresses dependence on a single representation while keeping the final fusion simple enough to inspect. It does not assume that one source, expert, or molecular property is sufficient for all endpoints.

Our objective is to test whether this combination improves external antibody ranking, identify the sources responsible for any benefit, and determine where the approach remains unreliable. We compare AbDev-X with matched components and PLM baselines, place its assay-level results beside verified published references, and examine split strategy, source removal, data efficiency, and interpretation. We also retain all ten measured endpoints and separate format-transfer analyses. This design links the proposed benefit---better use of complementary information in a small-data setting---to both its evidence and its practical limits.

'''
s=s[:s.index(r'\section{Background}')]+background+s[s.index(r'\section{Results}'):]

def replace_between(start,end,text):
    global s
    a=s.index(start);b=s.index(end,a)
    s=s[:a]+text+'\n\n'+s[b:]

replace_between(r'\subsection{Complementary experts improve external antibody ranking}',r'\figpanel{fig01_architecture_user.pdf}',r'''\subsection{Assay-aware experts improve aggregate ranking on external antibodies}
We first asked whether combining distinct assay-aware predictors improves ranking beyond using one representation or one expert alone. AbDev-X receives paired variable-region sequences, subtype metadata, and a structure-informed bio block, then combines four predictions with equal weights (Figure~\ref{fig:architecture}). E1 selects a representation using inner out-of-fold performance; A-MGFS restricts descriptors through assay-related keyword masks; D-Stack combines PLM and descriptor/bio prediction streams; and F-MoE combines eligible mechanistic feature-group predictors. The experts therefore express different modeling assumptions before their outputs are averaged.

On GDPa3, AbDev-X achieved the highest recorded mean correlation among nine evaluated strategies across HIC, AC-SINS, PR-CHO, SMAC, and HAC: $\rho=.472$ (joint 95\% CI $[.349,.576]$). D-Stack, the strongest individual expert, reached $.452$, E1 reached $.444$, ESM-2 Ridge $.307$, and AbLang2 Ridge $.273$ (Supplementary Figure~\ref{fig:primary}A; Table~\ref{tab:components}). These comparisons use the same endpoints and external antibodies. The ensemble advantage is $.020$ over the strongest expert and $.165$ and $.199$ over the two PLM baselines in the mean correlation; these are descriptive point differences, not separate tests of the cross-assay mean. Thus, the improvement is associated with the combination of prediction strategies rather than a larger pretrained sequence model.''')

replace_between(r'The benefit has a clear assay-level scope.',r'\subsection{Benchmark context separates aggregate gains from assay-specialist performance}',r'''The individual results reveal what the aggregate represents. Correlations were $.547$ for HIC, $.262$ for AC-SINS, $.251$ for PR-CHO, $.647$ for SMAC, and $.650$ for HAC. Each recorded BCa 95\% interval excluded zero, although the AC-SINS lower limit was close to zero. We refer to these endpoints as the five positive-signal assays because of their observed results; they were not a prospectively registered subset. AbDev-X led the nine-strategy comparison individually on HIC and HAC, while other experts gave larger points on several remaining assays. Its aggregate benefit therefore comes from combining useful predictions across endpoints without requiring one expert to win every assay.

The complete ten-endpoint evaluation is essential to interpreting this finding. Applying the unchanged procedure to Tm1, Tm2, Tonset, SEC, and Titer yielded weak or uncertain correlations and reduced the all-endpoint mean to $.249$ (Supplementary Figure~\ref{fig:primary}B; Table~\ref{tab:allten}). The five-assay result establishes a useful observed regime for this ensemble, while the ten-assay result tests the stronger proposition that the same procedure can predict all developability endpoints. The evidence supports the former.''')

replace_between(r'\subsection{Benchmark context separates aggregate gains from assay-specialist performance}',r'\figpanel{main07_baseline_context.pdf}',r'''\subsection{Published comparisons locate the gain within the assay landscape}
An aggregate advantage over matched local models does not establish an advantage over every published assay specialist. We therefore compared the current predictions with verified GDPa reference points and retained the corresponding evaluation scope (Figure~\ref{fig:baseline_main}; Table~\ref{tab:published}). Jiang\,et\,al. provide the most directly relevant published comparison: three fixed models evaluated on seven matching GDPa3 endpoints~\citep{jiang2026}. Their Supporting Table S3 supplies those points; mapped titer in Table S4 is a separate sensitivity comparison. AbDev-X exceeds their largest assay-level points for AC-SINS, PR-CHO, Tm1, and Tonset, but is below them for HIC, Tm2, and SEC.

Across those same seven endpoints, AbDev-X has mean correlation $.151$ (joint 95\% CI $[.068,.230]$), compared with $.126$, $.139$, and $.125$ for Jiang's three fixed models. This common-endpoint comparison is more informative than comparing the $.472$ five-assay mean with a seven-assay literature mean. Published prediction vectors and matched comparator intervals are unavailable, so the differences remain descriptive. Competition champions also retain larger points on the five competition assays~\citep{ginkgo2026}. The evidence consequently positions AbDev-X as an effective combination of the evaluated local predictors, with particular assay gains, rather than a replacement for all specialist models.

Other studies establish methodological context rather than numerical superiority tests. Amini\,et\,al. use internal Biogen panels~\citep{amini2026}, PROPERMAB studies HIC and high-concentration viscosity on its own datasets~\citep{propermab2025}, and DELPHI is a software platform whose stored configurations do not supply a matched published benchmark here~\citep{delphi}. Their contributions motivate representation choice and practical deployment, but their scores cannot be placed on a common GDPa3 leaderboard without reproducing the relevant protocols. The separate historical baseline/scatter/CV-distribution graphic is discussed in Supplementary Figure~\ref{fig:performance_main}.''')

replace_between(r'\subsection{Assay-level comparisons define where the ensemble is useful}',r'\subsection{Distribution shift reveals why representation choice must remain assay aware}',r'''\subsection{The clearest benefits concern hydrophobic and surface-interaction ranking}
For early candidate prioritization, the relevant question is which property predictions improve enough to change the evidence available for a shortlist. HIC provides one clear matched-baseline gain: AbDev-X reaches $.547$, compared with $.363$ for ESM-2 and $.229$ for AbLang2. SMAC and HAC broaden the useful assay coverage, with AbDev-X reaching $.647$ and $.650$, compared with ESM-2 points of $.386$ and $.420$. Paired BCa intervals for differences from both PLM baselines exclude zero for all three assays (Table~\ref{tab:paired}); these exploratory comparisons are not adjusted for multiplicity. This is the strongest evidence that the ensemble adds ranking information beyond frozen sequence embeddings.

The benefit has a different character for self-association and polyreactivity. AC-SINS and PR-CHO reach $.262$ and $.251$, above Jiang's largest published points of $.235$ and $.116$, but the matched differences from the local PLMs are not resolved by the recorded intervals. Individual experts also retain useful specialization: E1/AbLang2 give the strongest AC-SINS point ($.284$), F-MoE the strongest PR-CHO point ($.301$), and D-Stack the strongest SMAC point ($.657$) within the nine-strategy comparison. These results explain the value of retaining several experts while showing why an average can trade a small loss on one endpoint for better aggregate ranking. They do not support replacing a validated assay specialist solely because the ensemble has the largest mean.

The weaker endpoints test the limits of the same design. Tm2 gives $-.107$ (BCa 95\% CI $[-.348,.122]$), below both the local ESM-2 point ($.080$) and Jiang's three published points ($.110$, $.127$, and $.041$). SEC is also lower than Jiang's best point ($-.229$ versus $-.058$). Tm1 ($.200$) and Tonset ($.132$) are numerically above Jiang's largest points ($.121$ and $.089$), but their own intervals include zero; Titer remains uncertain ($.133$). These outcomes show that combining the available predictors does not by itself recover missing or poorly transferring endpoint signal. They also prevent the surface-interaction gains from being misinterpreted as validated thermal, monomer-content, or expression prediction.

In practical terms, the current evidence supports testing AbDev-X for ranking candidates on HIC, SMAC, and HAC in a population comparable to GDPa3, while retaining the other measured properties as independent selection requirements. It does not establish an absolute pass/fail threshold, prospective enrichment, or a reduction in experimental cost. Those are deployment questions that require a predefined candidate-selection study.''')

replace_between(r'\subsection{Distribution shift reveals why representation choice must remain assay aware}',r'\figpanel{main03_generalization.pdf}',r'''\subsection{Split and representation comparisons explain why assay specificity matters}
We next asked why a single representation is insufficient. If the apparent gains reflected only related antibodies crossing validation folds, they would offer limited evidence for ranking new families. Under random versus cluster--isotype CV, primary-model AC-SINS correlation decreased from $.586$ to $.505$ and PR-CHO from $.586$ to $.431$, whereas HIC changed little ($.693$ to $.686$; Figure~\ref{fig:generalization_main}A). The split therefore affects endpoints unequally. Cluster--isotype CV separates sequence clusters while balancing subclass composition; it is not leave-one-isotype-out validation. GDPa3 remains the external assessment of whether development ranking transfers.

The CV-to-held-out comparison exposes a second distinction: a source can look useful during development without carrying the same signal into the external panel (B). Tm2 and Titer illustrate this limitation. The standalone PLM comparison also changes by assay (C), while historical ridge source coefficients give different weight to descriptors, subtype, and antibody embeddings (D). These coefficients belong to the stated source-stack experiment rather than the primary final average. Together, the panels support the design premise of assay-dependent source use and explain why neither a favorable random split nor a single encoder ranking is sufficient for model selection.''')

replace_between(r'A broader encoder and structural-feature screen sharpened this interpretation',r'\figpanel{main04_encoder_structure.pdf}',r'''The broader encoder and structural-feature screen asks which information could supply that complementarity (Figure~\ref{fig:encoder_main}). Neither antibody-specific pretraining nor increased encoder size guarantees the strongest result across the competition assays. CDR and whole-variable-region inputs also differ by endpoint, indicating that pooling strategy changes the information retained. In the standalone structural screen, combined features reach HIC $\rho\simeq.66$, while Tm2 shows weak structural signal. Predicted structural context is therefore promising for surface-related behavior, but adding it is not a universal remedy for difficult endpoints. These screens motivate the expert inputs; their five-assay subset includes Tm2 and Titer and must be distinguished from the five positive-signal assays used for the primary component comparison.''')

replace_between(r'\subsection{Matched source ablations locate the useful complementarity}',r'\figpanel{main05_efficiency.pdf}',r'''\subsection{Source removal connects the ensemble gains to complementary information}
The representation screens identify plausible sources of signal, but a source can perform well alone without helping the primary ensemble. We therefore removed each representation block and refitted the procedure (Figure~\ref{fig:efficiency_main}A). Removing the bio block lowered held-out HIC correlation by approximately $.150$ and SMAC by $.208$ in the primary five-assay analysis. Removing AbLang2 lowered AC-SINS by $.112$. These changes connect the gains to information beyond a uniform frozen-PLM predictor and support the intended division between sequence and physicochemical routes.

The ablations also identify an unfavorable contribution. Removing ESM-2 improved PR-CHO by approximately $.054$. Additional information can therefore impair external ranking, even when the full ensemble has the best aggregate point. This result gives a concrete target for future refinement: test whether development-only source exclusion can identify such dependencies without using the external outcomes to select the model. The held-out ablation is explanatory evidence, not a license to retune the primary result on GDPa3.

Learning curves address the related question of whether more assay-matched data could change these trade-offs (B). Performance generally improves with training fraction, with intermediate decreases or plateaus in some settings. The curves support collecting additional measurements but do not establish monotonic improvement or an absence of saturation for every endpoint. Paired held-out comparisons (C and Table~\ref{tab:paired}) locate the clearest PLM-baseline gains on HIC, SMAC, and HAC; AC-SINS and PR-CHO remain less certain. The CDR-enrichment analysis (D) is a separate chain-aware network diagnostic that suggests regional sequence signal, rather than an attribution of AbDev-X itself.

Additional ablations test whether a more flexible combination is automatically preferable. Historical soft-gating, physical-constraint, and multitask experiments show mixed assay effects (Supplementary Figure~\ref{fig:soft_extensions}); routing and regional-pooling screens likewise fail to yield one uniformly best configuration (Supplementary Figure~\ref{fig:routing_unique}). Their protocols differ from the primary experiment, so they inform the design questions rather than establish a matched causal advantage for equal averaging. Primary expert refits and source-selection stability (Supplementary Figure~\ref{fig:expert_specialization}) further distinguish internal specialization from the fixed final weights.''')

replace_between(r'\subsection{Physicochemical explanations connect assay signal to molecular descriptors}',r'\figpanel{main06_shap_structure.pdf}',r'''\subsection{Physicochemical analyses give molecular context to the assay-dependent signal}
Source ablations identify where prediction information enters the model; interpretation asks what measured molecular attributes might underlie that information. LightGBM proxy SHAP analysis~\citep{lightgbm,shap} highlights hydrophobicity-related features for HIC and charge-related features for AC-SINS, with regional composition and liability motifs contributing to other endpoints (Figure~\ref{fig:shap_main}A). These patterns are consistent with the different properties sampled by the assays and provide hypotheses for examining candidates with unfavorable predictions.

The predicted-Fv surface panels place selected descriptor fields in molecular context (B--C). They show where hydrophobic exposure, aromatic exposure, or charge-related fields occur on a representative antibody. This supports inspection of plausible liabilities but does not establish that the highlighted residues caused the primary ensemble output. The proxy, displayed surface field, and AbDev-X are distinct functions; PR-CHO's illustrative distributed field is likewise not proof of a distributed molecular mechanism. Direct attribution would require analysis of the actual prediction function and a stated reference for changing sequences or structure-informed features.''')

replace_between(r'The descriptor-only Integrated Gradients analysis',r'\figpanel{main08_descriptor_interpretability.pdf}',r'''A complementary descriptor-only Integrated Gradients analysis~\citep{ig} resolves contributions by feature category and region (Figure~\ref{fig:descriptor}). The assay-dependent balance of hydrophobic, charge, polar, and size-related contributions is consistent with the need for selective source use. Its top-15 saved feature lists provide partial summaries, so the displayed category shares need not sum to one. Detailed proxy feature importance and per-antibody category contributions show that the dominant descriptor family also varies among antibodies (Supplementary Figure~\ref{fig:detailed_shap}). The combined interpretation is therefore a set of assay- and candidate-specific hypotheses for follow-up, rather than a universal sequence-editing rule.''')

discussion=r'''\section{Discussion}
AbDev-X addresses a practical small-data problem: several representations contain useful information, but their value changes across developability assays and may change again on external antibodies. The main finding is that four complementary assay-aware predictors give the highest observed mean GDPa3 correlation among the nine evaluated strategies on the five positive-signal endpoints. The gain over the strongest individual expert is modest ($.020$ in mean correlation), while the gains over the two frozen-PLM baselines are larger. Paired endpoint comparisons and source removal place the clearest evidence on HIC, SMAC, and HAC. This combination of matched performance and source-specific evidence supports a more precise contribution than an unrestricted claim of superior developability prediction.

The design offers one response to the limitations identified in earlier work. PROPERMAB establishes that engineered sequence and structural features can be valuable~\citep{propermab2025}; Jiang\,et\,al. show that adding embeddings does not guarantee a gain~\citep{jiang2026}; and Amini\,et\,al. demonstrate the relevance of sequence-family separation and training composition~\citep{amini2026}. AbDev-X places several ways of using those sources in parallel. Source selection permits a simpler view to remain competitive, descriptor masking restricts the information admitted for an assay, and stacking or feature-group prediction combines complementary views. The final average avoids an additional antibody-dependent gate. Our results support this evaluated construction; they do not isolate equal averaging as universally optimal or establish a new general law of ensemble diversity.

The biological interpretation follows from the contrasts between endpoints. Surface-related features contribute substantially to HIC and SMAC, and antibody embeddings contribute to AC-SINS. Conversely, the PR-CHO source-removal result shows that an extra representation can introduce a dependency that transfers poorly. The useful principle is therefore conditional complementarity: retaining multiple sources makes it possible to exploit different kinds of signal, but their inclusion must still be tested for the target assay. Regional pooling, alternative learners, and regional descriptor constructions in the supplementary studies reinforce this point without supplying independent primary-model wins (Supplementary Figures~\ref{fig:routing_unique} and \ref{fig:learner_unique}).

The likely application is prioritization within a measured developability profile. For a candidate set similar to GDPa3, stronger HIC, SMAC, and HAC ranking could help focus follow-up measurements on hydrophobic or surface-interaction liabilities while binding, thermal stability, monomer content, and expression remain separately assessed. A useful workflow would retain the individual assay predictions and their validation evidence, rather than merge them into a clinical-success score. This is a proposed use of the model, not a demonstrated saving in experiments or manufacturing cost. The historical composite-ranking study, where enrichment changes with shortlist size, illustrates why prospective selection utility needs its own evaluation (Supplementary Figure~\ref{fig:transfer_unique}).

The weaker results are informative because they test a stronger version of the same hypothesis. If available representation diversity were sufficient for all endpoints, the unchanged ensemble should also transfer on Tm2 and SEC. It does not. AbDev-X is below the verified Jiang points on both endpoints and does not exceed the strongest published HIC specialist. Higher points for Tm1 and Tonset do not establish reliable prediction because their own intervals include zero. These findings identify assays that require additional endpoint-specific development rather than showing that AbDev-X wins through a different metric. They also distinguish improved aggregate ranking from validated prediction of all ten properties. A future model should address those failures with development-only decisions and independent evaluation, rather than removing difficult endpoints from its reported scope.

Several constraints shape the strength of the conclusion. The five positive-signal endpoints form a retrospective grouping, the external panel is small, and assay-level sample sizes differ. GDPa1 includes multiple subclasses whereas GDPa3 is an IgG1 panel; sequence novelty and composition shift therefore occur together. Bootstrap intervals quantify uncertainty conditional on the evaluated predictions and sampling scheme, not all sources of model-development or population uncertainty. The paired comparisons are exploratory and unadjusted for multiplicity. Published point comparisons lack matched prediction vectors and cannot establish statistical superiority. These constraints are particularly relevant to the small difference from D-Stack and to the uncertain self-association and polyreactivity gains.

Interpretation and transfer impose further requirements. Proxy SHAP, descriptor IG, and surface maps help formulate hypotheses, but direct molecular explanations require attributions of the actual ensemble and experimental perturbation. Bispecific arm averaging and VHH-Fc transfer operate under different dependencies and candidate distributions; their historical results cannot be substituted for a format-matched AbDev-X validation. Similarly, predictor disagreement is not established as calibrated confidence, and augmentation effects differ by assay (Supplementary Figures~\ref{fig:soft_extensions}, \ref{fig:transfer_unique}, and \ref{fig:confidence_unique}). Context-aware adaptation offers a complementary research direction~\citep{camap2026}, but must be compared on experimental endpoints under matched conditions.

A prospective test would predefine the candidate population, assay panel, favorable directions, and shortlist budget; freeze the complete prediction procedure before measurement; and evaluate both assay ranking and enrichment of experimentally acceptable candidates. Reporting the weaker endpoints alongside the stronger ones would test whether an improved profile produces better decisions. Within that scope, the present results establish that assay-aware use of complementary predictors is a useful alternative to relying on a single frozen representation, and identify the information and endpoints that should guide its next development.

\section{Conclusions}
AbDev-X improves aggregate external ranking across five observed positive-signal assays by combining source selection, descriptor masking, prediction stacking, and mechanistic feature-group models. Matched comparisons and ablations show the clearest benefits for hydrophobic and surface-interaction endpoints, while the complete evaluation exposes unresolved thermal, SEC, and expression prediction. The contribution is an assay-aware framework for combining existing molecular information with an explicit evaluation scope. Its practical impact should be tested through prospective, assay- and format-matched candidate prioritization.
'''
replace_between(r'\section{Discussion}',r'\section{Methods}',discussion)

newrefs=[
r'\bibitem{hie2024} Hie BL, Shanker VR, Xu D, Bruun TUJ, Weidenbacher PA, Tang S, et al. Efficient evolution of human antibodies from general protein language models. Nat Biotechnol. 2024;42:275--283. \url{https://doi.org/10.1038/s41587-023-01763-2}.',
r'\bibitem{propermab2025} Li B, Luo S, Wang W, Xu J, Liu D, Shameem M, et al. PROPERMAB: an integrative framework for in silico prediction of antibody developability using machine learning. mAbs. 2025;17:2474521. \url{https://doi.org/10.1080/19420862.2025.2474521}.',
r'\bibitem{camap2026} Giancardo L, Yilmaz M, Lee E, Ren K, Zhao Y, Trang G, et al. Context-aware multi-property antibody predictor: a novel framework integrating text and protein language models. NPJ Syst Biol Appl. 2026;12:95. \url{https://doi.org/10.1038/s41540-026-00723-1}.',
]
# Author strings for new entries are independently checked before compilation.
s=s.replace(r'\end{thebibliography}', '\n'.join(newrefs)+'\n'+r'\end{thebibliography}')
bm=re.search(r'\\begin\{thebibliography\}\{[^}]+\}(.*?)\\end\{thebibliography\}',s,re.S)
entries=re.findall(r'\\bibitem\{([^}]+)\}(.*?)(?=\\bibitem|\Z)',bm.group(1),re.S)
refs={k:v.strip() for k,v in entries}
order=[]
for cit in re.findall(r'\\cite\w*\{([^}]+)\}',s):
    for k in cit.split(','):
        if k not in order: order.append(k)
missing=set(order)-refs.keys()
assert not missing,missing
order += [k for k in refs if k not in order]
bib='\n'+'\n'.join(r'\bibitem{'+k+'} '+refs[k] for k in order)+'\n'
s=s[:bm.start(1)]+bib+s[bm.end(1):]
dest=OUT/'AbDevX_evidence_led_narrative_main_SI.tex'
dest.write_text(s,encoding='utf-8')
fignames=re.findall(r'\\(?:figpanel|widepanel)\{([^}]+)',s)
checks={'source':str(SRC),'figure_references':fignames,'figures_unchanged':all((OUT/'figures'/f).read_bytes()==(SRC/'figures'/f).read_bytes() for f in fignames),'reference_count':len(refs),'missing_citations':sorted(missing)}
# Compare the supplement directly, including figure discussion and numerical tables.
checks['supplement_preserved']=s.split(r'\section{Supplementary Information}',1)[1]==(SRC/'AbDevX_SI_discussion_main_SI.tex').read_text(encoding='utf-8').split(r'\section{Supplementary Information}',1)[1]
(OUT/'evidence'/'narrative_audit.json').write_text(json.dumps(checks,indent=2),encoding='utf-8')
print(dest)
print(checks)
